{#
    Tabs, as the element that already knows how to be a tablist.

    The core renders a `<ul>` of buttons and every panel underneath it, and
    leaves the switching to a script the renderer does not ship — so both
    panels are on the page at once and neither button does anything. This is
    the same content handed to `sds-tabs`, which draws the bar and wires the
    arrow keys.

    **The labels are a property.** A set of tabs is the one component here that
    cannot be told what it holds by the markup around it — the labels sit on
    the items inside it, and an element rendered before the browser has no
    items to read them off. So they are said once, on the set, the way every
    other option in this theme is said. The panels are still written between
    the tags, because a panel is a section of the document and there is no
    attribute that holds one.
#}
{%- set labels = [] -%}
{%- for tab in node.tabs -%}
    {%- set labels = labels|merge([renderNode(tab.content)|striptags|trim]) -%}
{%- endfor -%}
<sds-tabs items="{{ labels|json_encode }}">
    {%- for tab in node.tabs %}
        <sds-tab-item label="{{ labels[loop.index0] }}">
            {{- renderNode(tab.value) -}}
        </sds-tab-item>
    {%- endfor %}
</sds-tabs>
